WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.740
ChatGPT works agent tools, finally get a clear map from a builder who tested them end-to-end. Simon Willis

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:12.600
and published a hands-on explanation of what the platform can actually do. He describes it as deeply

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.500
confusing, yet extremely powerful, with features unavailable in regular chatGPT. The post walks through

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:25.340
tool access and agent spawning that differ sharply from the standard interface. A follow-up prompt

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:31.280
produced a live reference site cataloging every tool with descriptions. The work clarifies how

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:37.520
work supports collaboration patterns that standard chatGPT does not expose. Builders working with

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:43.760
OpenAI's agent features now have a concrete starting point for experimentation. Watch for further

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:50.020
community testing of the spawn underscore agent behavior across accounts. This is Models and Agents,

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:56.640
Episode 159. Simon Willis and also released an auto-generated reference site listing every

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:03.200
available tool in chatGPT work. The post-highlight differences like the collaboration spawn agent tool

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:09.360
that exists in work but not regular chat. Builders exploring agentic workflows should review

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:16.160
the linked resources this week. A new paper accelerates LLM inference via vector index-based output

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:22.960
embeddings. It introduces an HNSW-based vector index that replaces dense vocabulary projection

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:30.880
during autoregressive decoding. On CPU-infinance with Gemma 3, LLM 3.2, and QN-3 models,

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:38.700
the method improves end-to-end batch size-1 throughput by up to 82% for Gemma 3-270 million while

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:44.840
preserving alpaca-eval quality. The approach retrieves only high scoring token candidates and

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:51.000
scatters lojits into a sparse tensor. Builders running small batch CPU inference should test the

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.880
integration on their current Gemma or LLMOS setups this week. Another paper examines the effect of

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:03.500
emotional context on large language models endorsement of premature decisions. Six commercial models were

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:09.500
tested across career, business, and immigration scenarios under neutral versus distress conditions.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:16.140
Emotional expression raised endorsement scores from 18.6 to 31.5 on average,

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:22.560
with five of the six models showing significant effects. Claude Opus was the only model that did not

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:28.240
shift. Teams building decision support agents should add emotional context testing to their

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:35.300
evaluation suites. A third paper shows the benefits of modular entity disambiguation with LLM-based

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:43.660
selection without training. A training-free BM-25 retriever paired with an LLM selector reached 86.3

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:52.100
in KB Micro F1 on the Zelda benchmark, improving on the prior 82.3. Adding a trained dense retriever

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:59.420
pushed the score to 88.5. The framework also supports abstention when retrieval fails. Developers

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:05.780
maintaining knowledge graphs should evaluate the BM-25 plus LLM pipeline before investing in

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:12.180
retriever training. Inspire offers an internalized then-improve approach for example driven mathematical

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.940
reasoning. The method first uses reference guided student internalization to create preference

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:24.380
candidates, then applies staged rubric training. Experiments across model scales showed consistent

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:30.440
gains and no degradation on out-of-distribution benchmarks. Teams working on mathematical reasoning

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:36.200
agents can adopt the two-stage preference pipeline. Trajectory level speculative decoding for

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:43.440
diffusion language models reduces denoising iterations by 30-40%. The framework constructs draft denoising

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:49.100
trajectories via confidence stratified tree exploration and verifies them with blockwise parallel

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:57.180
evaluation. It raises tokens per step from 2.6 to 4.3 delivering 7 to 14 times speed up over

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:02.640
vanilla diffusion LMs. Builders experimenting with diffusion language models should integrate

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:08.460
the dual cache approach. An article examines real production deployments of multi-agent systems

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:14.240
and the coordination patterns that succeed at scale. It focuses on how teams move from prototype

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:20.180
to reliable shipping workflows. Operations leaders evaluating agent meshes should read the case

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:26.200
details for deployment checklists. This builds directly on yesterday's coverage of agent tool use

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:31.720
by showing which coordination patterns actually reach production. Kindrel in Google Cloud

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:38.880
advanced agentic AI at InCore Bank. The partnership deploys agentic AI capabilities inside a banking

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:45.080
environment using Google Cloud infrastructure. The work targets specific operational processes at

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:51.360
InCore Bank. Financial services teams exploring agentic automation can review the announced scope

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:58.100
for comparable use cases. A paper asks what makes agent memory useful for reliable, unanswerable

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:04.780
question handling. Four memory methods were evaluated across three UAQ data sets and two base models

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:11.200
under a unified agentic rag framework. Gaines proved selective and fragile under data set shift

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:15.200
with procedural and rule-based memories providing the most reliable support.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:21.260
Agent developers should prioritize decision guidance memory formats over raw trajectory storage.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.220
Entity memory graph retrieval improves evidence coverage in long conversation question answering.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:35.980
Graph retrieval on 1,986 questions from 10 locomo conversations raised evidence recall at top K-25

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:47.020
from 79.75% to 84.48%. The gain held across cutoffs from 5 to 50 while final answer F1 showed no consistent

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:52.760
difference. Teams building long conversation agents should test graph structured retrieval for evidence

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:59.020
coverage. Willisson ran a prompt that produced a live site listing every chat GPT work tool with

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:04.180
descriptions. The site serves as a practical companion to his main explanation post.

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:09.020
Developers mapping works capabilities can browse the generated reference directly.

